    <project label="unplugged-functions">
      <title>Unplugged: Functions</title>
      <statement>
     <p>
      In groups, choose one person to be the function. The person playing
      the function should choose a secret math formula with a variable and 2 operations,
      for example <c>2*x + 1</c>. Don't tell the rest of the group what the formula is! 
     </p>
     <p>
      The other members of the group will give the
      function different argument values for <c>x</c>, and the function will
      return the result of the formula. Write down the input/output pairs for each argument 
      and return value.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, if the function's formula is <c>2*x + 1</c> and a group
      member provides <c>3</c> as the argument, the function returns <c>7</c>.
      After each member gets a return value, the group should try to figure
      out the function's secret formula. Then, discuss and answer the following 
      questions as a group.
    </p>
      <ol>
        <li>What arguments did your group provide to the function? 
        What return value did the function produce for each argument?
          </li>
        <li>What was the function's secret formula? 
          How did your group use the input/output pairs to try to figure it out?</li>
         <li>How does this activity demonstrate that a function can be reused with different arguments?</li>
         <li>Can you use a function without knowing how it works inside? 
          How does this activity demonstrate that a function is an abstraction like a "black box"?</li>
      </ol>
  </statement>
    <response/>
    </project>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="function-call-defn">
    <title>Procedure/Function Calls and Definitions</title>
    <idx>function call</idx>
    <idx>function definition</idx>
    <idx>procedure call</idx>
    <idx>procedure definition</idx>

  <p>Let's learn how to define our own funtions and call them to do their jobs!
    The main reasons to divide up a program into multiple functions are to organize the code to reduce its complexity and 
    to avoid repetition of code. 
    For example, the "This Old Man" song below has a lot of repetition    
    (<url href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Di23O5cN4ZU">listen here</url>).
    Instead of writing the same code over and over in order to print the song, we can write functions that for different
    parts of the song and run them when needed. Click on all lines of code that are repeated in the song below, and then we will learn how they can be replaced with a function called <c>chorus()</c>.
  </p>
    <activity label="repeatedcode-this-old-man">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Click on all the lines that are completely identical and repeated.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <feedback>
    <p>Look for lines of Python code that are completely identical.</p>
  </feedback>

  <areas>
    <cline><area correct="no">print("This old man, he played one.")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="no">print("He played knick-knack on my thumb.")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="yes">print("With a knick-knack paddywhack, give a dog a bone,")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="yes">print("This old man came rolling home.")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="no">print("This old man, he played two.")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="no">print("He played knick-knack on my shoe.")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="yes">print("With a knick-knack paddywhack, give a dog a bone,")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="yes">print("This old man came rolling home.")</area></cline>
  </areas>
</activity>

 <p>There are two steps to writing a function: the <term>function definition</term> and the <term>function call</term>. 
  A function in Python is defined by using the keyword <c>def</c> followed by the function name which the programmer chooses. 
  Indented inside the function, we write the code that will be executed when the function is called. 
  We can call a function or procedure by using its name, for example <c>chorus()</c>. Functions always have parentheses
 () after their name even if it's empty parentheses. In Python, functions always have to be defined before they can be called, so  
 programmers put the function definitions at the top of the program and the main program below.</p>

 <program language="python">
      <code>
  # Function definition
  def functionName():
      &lt;block of statements&gt;

  # Function call
  functionName() 
      </code>
    </program>

       <p>In AP CSP pseudocode, functions are called <term>procedures</term>, and the keyword <c>procedure</c> is used instead of <c>def</c> to define a function. Curly braces are used to enclose the block of statements, insead of just indentation. Procedure calls
      just use the name of the procedure with parentheses like in Python.</p>

    <program language="python">
      <code>
  # AP pseudocode procedure definition
  procedure procName()
  {
      &lt;block of statements&gt;
  }

  # AP pseudocode procedure call
  procName() 
      </code>
</program>
 
<note>
      <title>3.2.B.1</title>
      <p>
        Calling a procedure runs a named sequence of steps, which helps programs reuse
        code and keep the overall program easier to read and follow.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.B.2</title>
      <p>
        A procedure is called by using its name in a statement so the program runs the
        steps stored in that procedure. When the call happens, the program pauses the
        current sequence of statements, executes the procedure's instructions, and then
        resumes at the next statement after the call.
      </p>
    </note>

<p>When a function is called to do its job, the computer jumps to the function definition and runs the code inside it. 
  When the function is finished, the computer jumps back to the line after the function call and continues running the program. 
  See how the <c>chorus()</c> function is called in the code below by clicking on the next button. Watch the red arrow as the program jumps to the function definition and then returns to the line after the function call. Notice how we have reduced repetition of
  code by abstracting the chorus into a function that can be called multiple times. 
</p>
<activity label="codelensSong1">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Click on the next button below the code to step through the code.
      Watch the red arrow as the program calls the <c>chorus()</c> function
      and then returns to the line where the function was called.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <program interactive="codelens" language="python">
    <code>
def chorus():
    print("With a knick-knack paddywhack, give a dog a bone,")
    print("This old man came rolling home.")


print("This old man, he played one.")
print("He played knick-knack on my thumb.")
chorus()

print("This old man, he played two.")
print("He played knick-knack on my shoe.")
chorus()
    </code>
  </program>
</activity>


  
    <note>
      <title>3.2.A.2</title>
      <p>
        If a procedure does not need any input information to complete its intended task,
        it can be defined with its name followed by parentheses and a block of statements.
        Each time the procedure is called, the statements in the procedure execute, and
        then the program continues with the next statement after the call.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.A.9</title>
      <p>
        Defining a procedure creates the named steps, but the code inside runs only when
        the procedure is called.
      </p>
    </note>
  
     <p>
      Try writing your own function calls below to reduce repetition of code and organize your program.
    </p>

    <activity label="song-add-calls">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Replace the repeated chorus lines in the code below with a function call to <c>chorus()</c> to reduce repetition of code. 
        </p>
      </statement>

      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
def chorus():
    print("With a knick-knack paddywhack, give a dog a bone,")
    print("This old man came rolling home.")

# Main program
print("This old man, he played one.")
print("He played knick-knack on my thumb.")
chorus()

print("This old man, he played two.")
print("He played knick-knack on my shoe.")
# Replace the lines below with a chorus function call
print("With a knick-knack paddywhack, give a dog a bone,")
print("This old man came rolling home.")

print("This old man, he played three.")
print("He played knick-knack on my knee.")
# Replace the lines below with a chorus function call
print("With a knick-knack paddywhack, give a dog a bone,")
print("This old man came rolling home.")
        </code>
        <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
        count = self.getEditorText().count("chorus()")
        self.assertLessEqual(4, count, "Call chorus() at least twice more")
        count = self.getEditorText().count("With a knick-knack paddywhack")
        self.assertEqual(1, count, "Use chorus() instead of repeated lines \"With a knick-knack paddywhack\"")

myTests().main()
          </tests>
          </program>
          </activity>

    <activity label="songMethodsTrace">
